The limit can also be found directly, without memorizing the three cases, as done here.

This calculation uses the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}h(x)
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{x^2+6}{x^2+3}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{x^2+6}{x^{2}}}{\dfrac{x^2+3}{x^{2}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide both sides by }x^{2}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{\cancel{x^2}}{\cancel{x^2}}+\dfrac{6}{x^2}}{\dfrac{\cancel{x^2}}{\cancel{x^2}}+\dfrac{3}{x^2}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{1+\dfrac{6}{x^2}}{1+\dfrac{3}{x^2}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1+0}{1+0}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=1
\end{align}$
